Q: Not able to validate the error tooltip as it does not show in DOM A: <p>I see a few options:</p>
<ol>
<li>Do a screenshot test to see if the tooltip is present. This is slow and heavy, but it's possible.</li>
<li>Assert on the Angular classes. When there's text added, <code>ng-invalid</code> changes to <code>ng-valid</code>. This isn't user visible, but is fast.</li>
<li>Submit the form and assert that the URL is the same. This doesn't directly validate the error tooltip, but it's worth mentioning.</li>
</ol>
<p>Here's an example of all 3:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import {expect, test} from &quot;@playwright/test&quot;; // ^1.53.0

const url = &quot;https://demo.automationtesting.in/Register.html&quot;;

test(&quot;first name input validation failure&quot;, async ({page}) =&gt; {
  await page.goto(url, {waitUntil: &quot;commit&quot;});
  const firstName = page.getByPlaceholder(&quot;First Name&quot;);
  const firstNameClass =
    &quot;form-control ng-pristine ng-untouched ng-invalid ng-invalid-required&quot;;
  await expect(firstName).toHaveClass(firstNameClass);
  await page.locator(&quot;#submitbtn&quot;).click();
  await expect(firstName).toHaveClass(firstNameClass);
  await expect(page.locator(&quot;form&quot;)).toHaveScreenshot();
  await expect(page).toHaveURL(url);
});

test(&quot;first name input validation success&quot;, async ({page}) =&gt; {
  await page.goto(url, {waitUntil: &quot;commit&quot;});
  const firstName = page.getByPlaceholder(&quot;First Name&quot;);
  await expect(firstName).toHaveClass(
    &quot;form-control ng-pristine ng-untouched ng-invalid ng-invalid-required&quot;
  );
  await firstName.fill(&quot;testing&quot;);
  await expect(firstName).toHaveClass(
    &quot;form-control ng-untouched ng-dirty ng-valid-parse ng-valid ng-valid-required&quot;
  );
  await page.locator(&quot;#submitbtn&quot;).click();
  await expect(firstName).toHaveClass(
    &quot;form-control ng-dirty ng-valid-parse ng-valid ng-valid-required ng-touched&quot;
  );
  await expect(page.locator(&quot;form&quot;)).toHaveScreenshot();
});
</code></pre>
<p>Note that for the &quot;success&quot; test, you'll probably want to fill in the rest of the fields and assert <code>await expect(page).toHaveURL(&quot;success URL&quot;);</code>. However, the form seems <a href="https://github.com/webcompat/web-bugs/issues/140923" rel="nofollow noreferrer">broken</a> so I didn't bother with the rest of the test.</p>
<p>To emphasize: I don't necessarily <em>endorse</em> these approaches; just showing a few options to use at your discretion.</p>
